>> Is me a notmuch convention, or something built in, or just something you
>> came up with?
>
> I came up with it. notmuch has no notion of a current user in tags, and
> nothing resolves "me". The only convention is the "::" separator, which
> nmbug uses for its shared "notmuch::" prefix. To notmuch-git a prefix is
> only a filter: it commits the tags that start with it, and stores them
> with the prefix stripped, so "me::archived" lands in the repo as
> tags/<hash>/<msgid>/archived.
>
>> Because I wonder how this would work with multiple
>> people. Imagine I'm working with a team of people (or agents) and
>> everyone has their own archive tag. How does that work? Does me
>> automatically resolve to the current user? Or would we need individual
>> prefixes like pat, claude, etc.
>
> Neither is needed, because tags are not in the mail. They live in each
> reader's own notmuch database. Your database on beastie and mine on
> lab-claude share the maildir's messages and nothing else, so when I tag
> a message the tag exists only in my database. Each person's notmuch-git
> repo is private too. So every reader can use the same tag name, and the
> same mail-tag.sh line "-inbox -- tag:me::archived" works for all of
> them. "me::" reads as "mine, in whoever's database this is". If you
> prefer a plainer name, any fixed prefix works equally well; the choice
> only has to keep the personal tags apart from the derived ones.

I know I won't see your tags. I'm talking about persisting tags to the
git repo, which is what notmuch-git does afaik.

I use multiple machines and I would like to sync my tags. I also am
